<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Suhl</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Suhl is a town in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thuringian-forest">Thuringian Forest</a> in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thuringia">Thuringia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, known since the 16th century for gun-making and metalworking. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-germany">East Germany</a> it was the capital of the Suhl district from 1952 to 1990. Its weapons firms, among them Simson, made rifles, sporting guns and later motorcycles.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/suhl.jpg" alt="Suhl" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Thomas doerfer · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Suhl0030.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Suhl was first mentioned in a deed of 1318, though the annals of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fulda-abbey">Fulda Abbey</a> name a place called Sulaha at various points between 900 and 1155. Its coat of arms of 1365 shows two hammers, pointing to metal processing as its chief livelihood. The region belonged to the Franconian counts of Henneberg from the 11th century, and Suhl lay on a trade route from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gotha">Gotha</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erfurt">Erfurt</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arnstadt">Arnstadt</a> across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thuringian-forest">Thuringian Forest</a>. It became a small market town in 1445 and received full municipal rights in 1527. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a> was introduced in 1544.</p><p>Iron ore mining created the basis for a gunsmith trade, and metalworking boomed in the 16th century. When the Henneberg counts died out in 1583, Suhl passed to the Wettin electors of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a>, and it stayed a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> and later Prussian exclave within <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thuringia">Thuringia</a> for nearly 300 years. The boom ended in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-years-war">Thirty Years' War</a>, when Croat mercenaries under the imperial general Johann Ludwig Hektor von Isolani burnt the city down in 1634. From about 1690 Duke Moritz Wilhelm of Saxe-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeitz">Zeitz</a> supported its reconstruction as a mining town.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-vienna">Congress of Vienna</a> in 1815 gave Suhl to the Prussian Province of Saxony. The railway arrived in 1882, and industrialisation followed. After 1935 armaments production boomed again, and about 10,000 forced labourers worked in the city's arms industry after 1940. The US Army reached Suhl on 3 April 1945 and was replaced by Soviet troops on 1 July.</p><p>Metalworking led during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> to gunsmithing and armouring, and Suhl made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannon">cannons</a> throughout the 17th century and after, which many European powers used. J. P. Sauer und Sohn, maker of hunting rifles, shotguns and pistols, was based there for almost 200 years. Other firms included Simson, which made vehicles, the Waffenfabrik August Menz, which produced the small Liliput pistol in the 1920s, and C. G. Haenel.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Suhl lies on the south-western edge of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thuringian-forest">Thuringian Forest</a>, south-west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erfurt">Erfurt</a>, and is one of the six urban districts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thuringia">Thuringia</a>. Its population is about 37,000, down more than 35 per cent from 1988, after the city lost its administrative functions in 1990. The old town was largely demolished in the 1960s and rebuilt in concrete. It has a weapons museum, a vehicle museum devoted to Simson, and the Baroque St Mary's Church of 1753 to 1756.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Suhl” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1952 it was divided into the districts of Erfurt, Gera and <b>Suhl</b>; the state was re-established in 1990.</blockquote><a href="/state/free-state-of-thuringia">Free State of Thuringia</a></li></ul></section>
